{"kind":"assert","text":"START gate on AR-1 head c4af141f (on main e34c51df), for request ba2c2fdb and promise 13bdd599, answering 833510ac per your 6fad3324. One question for you below.\n\nDone:\n- Change 2. When the stored wake is in the future, #retryWake returns it, so the fallback never undercuts it.\n- Change 1, part one. After the terminal check, a pass whose stored wake is in the future returns retry with that wake. That pass makes no policy, target or provider call.\n- Change 3. The hour test now runs 12 cases: pending policy or missing target, each with and without the old wake, each in three modes. 'evicted' restarts the object before every wake. 'resident' keeps the object in memory. 'other-alarms' keeps it in memory and fires another owner's alarm every 30 s, and asserts that each of those passes makes no port call and leaves the alarm at the stored wake. All 12 pass. Paired control against d9bf1a5f: the 2 pending-policy 'other-alarms' cases fail with 'expected 2 to be 1' Directory calls. The other 10 pass there, because d9bf1a5f's missing-target path returned at the due check. Log: artroom-evidence/2026-11-16-ar1-gate/control-first-head-d9bf1a5f.log.\n- Change 4. The note states the rate.\n\nNot done, and the question: change 1, part two, 'a cleared wake returns idle'. I built it, and 17 of 210 archive tests failed. On an idle room, the full pass is where the archive notices work that no act signalled: a revoked policy or removed trust, which stops the archive (for example 'installed non-Grow Base room ... preserves coverage on trust removal'), and stored-row migrations after a deployment (for example 'missing v2 parent state seeds once', 'current-parent migration handles six and thirty-seven old passes'). This head therefore keeps the full pass for a cleared wake.\n\nCost: a confirmed, idle room makes up to about 6 Directory calls on each alarm from another owner, so M such alarms an hour on a Worker cost up to about 6 x M requests. The note says this